`imports_changed` fails to recognise a direct import of a changed module in two ordinary shapes, so `drives_subprocess` reports `True` for a test the selector *can* route, that test is dropped from `scored` (line 150), and its miss is never counted — silently inflating the measured recall, which is precisely the failure mode previous finding 1 was meant to close. (a) Parenthesised multi-line import: with text `from ddflow.services import (
    ci,
)
run_cli(repo)
` and `src=["ddflow/services/ci.py"]`, the check at line 176 is ` from\s+ddflow\.services\s+import\s[^
]* ci `; `[^
]*` cannot reach the `ci` on line 2, and the first alternative needs the literal `ddflow.services.ci` after `from`/`import`, so the function returns `False`, `drives_subprocess` returns `True`, and a test that imports the changed module directly is booked blind and excluded from the denominator. (b) Changed package init: `src=["ddflow/api/__init__.py"]` gives `module = "ddflow.api.__init__"` (line 173), a string that occurs in no import statement, so a test doing `import ddflow.api` or `from ddflow.api import review` (with `review` defined in the init) is likewise classified blind. The added unit test only exercises same-line forms (`from ddflow.services import ci`, line ~224), so it stays green while these routable misses are hidden.

